## ABSTRACT
Sluggish kinetics of the oxygen evolution reaction during the water splitting requires high-performance electrocatalysts with low cost and good stability. Metalorganic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted much attention as electrocatalysts owing to their unique properties. To improve their electrocatalytic activity and stability, we report a selenylation method to modulate the morphology and interfaces by forming flower-like MOF-selenide nanocomposites. The optimal sample exhibits high activity with an overpotential of $260~\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , much better than commercial ${\mathrm{RuO}}_{2}$ and the control samples. Moreover, the optimal sample has the lowest Tafel slope of $54.3\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec^{-1}}$ , indicating the fast reaction kinetics, which can be attributed to the lower charge-transfer resistance and high intrinsic activity. Chronoamperometry shows that the optimal sample can maintain the current density for $60\mathrm{~h~}$ at $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ after the initial surface reconstruction, demonstrating good stability. This study provides a new perspective on the development of highly efficient electrocatalysts based on polymetallic MOFs.
Keywords: Metal-organic frameworks, Solvothermal reaction, Selenylation, Oxygen evolution reaction, Electrocatalysts
## 1. Introduction
Energy shortage and environmental pollution are two major problems challenging us nowadays. To address these issues, it has gradually become a consensus to harness renewable energy by developing advanced energy conversion and storage systems [1,2]. Among them, electrochemical splitting water to produce eco-friendly hydrogen has become a research hotspot because of its high efficiency and zero-emission of carbon dioxide [3-8]. Nevertheless, the oxygen evolution reaction (OER) occurring at the anode of electrolyzer hinders the efficiency of water electrolysis, due to the notoriously sluggish kinetics [9-12]. Although precious metal catalysts, such as $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ , have been utilized as commercial catalysts, high cost and scarcity severely limit their large-scale applications [13,14]. There are ever-increasing demands for the development of high-efficient and cost-effective electrocatalysts toward OER [15-17].
Recently, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have been extensively explored as precursors for non-noble metal-based electrocatalysts, due to the substitutable earthabundant metal center, suitable organic ligands and tunable porosity structure [18-20]. By virtue of hydrothermal treatment or pyrolysis under certain atmospheres, MOFbased derivatives consisting of transition metal (TM) compounds can be obtained, as well as preserving the original morphology. A typical representation is the derived electrocatalysts from zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) [21,22]. For example, ZIF-derived ordered microporous carbon has been reported by Xia et al, to be used as bifunctional electrocatalysts for oxygen reduction and oxygen evolution in rechargeable zinc-air battery, which exhibits a high-power density of $221.1\ensuremath{\mathrm{\mW}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{\cm}}^{-2}$ as well as excellent stability during 500 discharging/charging cycles [23]. Moreover, precise heteroatom doping [24] and structure engineering [25] can also be achieved during the synthesis process [26]. Chen’s group introduced extra active metallic center into MOFs to remarkably improve the performance of electrocatalysts [27]. Su and coworkers selected MIL-88B-Fe as precursor and replaced $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ by $C0^{2+}$ to form bimetallic MOFs [28]. The as-prepared heterogeneity bimetallic MOFs exhibited superior OER performance with a low overpotential of $288~\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and Tafel slope of 39 mV dec-1. These studies suggest that MOFs are good alternatives to noble-metal electrocatalysts toward electrocatalysis.
Besides using MOFs as precursors, MOFs themselves have also been explored as electrocatalysts [29,30]. Early in 2016, Tang’s group reported ultrathin NiCo-MOF nanosheets directly used as promising electrocatalysts toward OER in alkaline solution and only required $250~\mathrm{mV}$ to obtain a current density of $10\ \mathrm{mA\cm^{-2}}$ [31]. They also found that the structural transformation of a $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}\mathrm{Co}_{0.5}$ -MOF-74 into $\mathrm{Ni_{0.5}C o_{0.5}O O H_{0.75}}$ with abundant oxygen vacancies during the OER by operando measurements [32]. The alternation of the geometric and electronic structure highly improves the electrocatalytic activity [33,34]. On the other hand, the introduction of heteroatoms into MOFs is also an effective strategy to boost the electrocatalytic activity. For instance, Lin’s group introduced Fe into MIL-53 structure to increase electrochemically-active areas as well as reaction sites and obtained superior OER activity with high current density $(50\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ ) at an overpotential of $233~\mathrm{mV}$ , a Tafel slope of $31.3~\mathrm{mV}$ dec-1 and excellent stability in alkaline solution [35]. Although the modulation from either intrinsic structure or extrinsic morphology have improved the electrocatalytic performance, but MOFs themselves usually suffer from inferior stability in polar solvents (e.g., aqueous solution) and the poor electrical conductivity [36]. It remains challengeable to obtain high-activity MOF-based electrocatalysts with good stability and conductivity.
Herein, we develop an in-situ selenylation method to not only adjust the morphology of MOF-74 but also to couple the selenides with MOFs together (denoted as $\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{y}\mathrm{Ni}_{z}\mathrm{Se}-$ MOF, where $x,y,$ and $z$ represent the mole ratio among Fe, Co and Ni), as shown in Figure 1a. A series of control experiments at different conditions reveal that the introduction of selenium plays a key role in modulating the morphology of MOF74. Meanwhile, the formation of transition metal selenide anchoring on MOF-74 would synergistically contributes to the electrocatalytic performance toward OER, due to the good electrical conductivity of selenides and the reconstruction of selenides after activation. The optimized sample $(\mathrm{Fe_{1}C o_{2}N i_{1}S e-M O F})$ exhibits an overpotential of only $260~\mathrm{\mV}$ at the current density of $10\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\cm}^{-2}$ in 1M KOH electrolyte, which outperforms the original pure MOF, $\mathrm{Fe_{1}C o_{2}N i_{1}S e}$ , commercial ${\mathrm{RuO}}_{2}$ and other $\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{y}\mathrm{Ni}_{z}\mathrm{Se}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{N}$ OF samples. Moreover, $\mathrm{Fe_{1}C o_{2}N i_{1}S e\mathrm{-}M O}$ F demonstrates the lowest Tafel slope of $54.3\mathrm{~mV~dec^{-1}}$ , indicating a fast reaction kinetics. After a stability measurement, the rod-shape morphology has also been remained accompanying with a rougher surface than the original one, which can be attributed the surface reconstruction caused by the activation. This works provides a new pathway of designing MOF-based nanocomposite by selenylation toward cost-effective OER electrocatalysts.
## 2. Experimental part
## 2.1 Chemicals
All agents can be used directly without further purification. Ferrous chloride tetrahydrate $\mathrm{(FeCl}_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , AR), Cobaltous nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ , AR), and Hydrazine hydrate $(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{N}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},85\%)$ , All purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent. Nickel acetate tetrahydrate $\mathrm{(Ni(CH_{3}C O O)_{2}{\cdot}4H_{2}O}$ , AR) is purchased from Sigma Aldrich. 2,5-Dihydroxyterephthalic acid ( $\mathrm{\R_{4}D O T}$ , $98\%$ ) and Selenium (Se, AR) were purchased from Macklin. N, N-Dimethylformamide (DMF, AR) purchased from Aladdin.
## 2.2 Synthesis of $F e_{x}C o_{y}N i_{z}S e{-}M O F$
Different molar ratios of $\mathrm{Co(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ , $\mathrm{FeCl}_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ ， $(\mathrm{Fe};\mathrm{Co};\mathrm{Ni}=x{:}y{:}z)$ and $\mathrm{H_{4}D O T}$ were dissolved in the mixture of $30~\mathrm{ml}$ DMF, $2~\mathrm{ml}$ ethanol, and $2~\mathrm{ml}$ water. Then the mixture was stirred at room temperature for 2 hours. Meanwhile, 0.6 mmol Se powder was dissolved in $0.5~\mathrm{ml}~85\%$ hydrazine hydrate and kept at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $0.5\mathrm{~h~}$ to form a uniform solution. The two uniform solutions were mixed and stirred for 2 hours to form a uniform mixture. Then it was transferred to 50 ml reactor and reacted for 24 hours at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . At last, the product was washed with ethanol and deionized water several times and was dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in vacuum for $10\mathrm{{h}}$ . For comparison, $\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{y}\mathrm{Ni}_{z}\mathrm{Se}$ samples were synthesized following by the abovementioned procedure without the addition of $\mathrm{H_{4}D O T}$ and DMF. $\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{y}\mathrm{Ni}_{z}$ -MOF samples were synthesized without the addition of the solution of Se powder and hydrazine hydrate.
## 2.3 Characterization
The crystal structure of the as-prepared samples was characterized by X-ray diffraction (Rigaku Ultima IV X-ray diffractometer equipped with Cu Kα radiation) at room temperature. The morphology and elemental mapping of the samples were observed by field emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM, JEOLJSM-7800F), with energy dispersive spectrometer (EDS). The crystal structure of the materials was characterized by high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (TEM, JEOLJEM2100). X-ray photoelectron characterization was recorded on a Thermo scalable 250Xi electron spectrometer system (XPS) using Al Kα radiation. The specific surface area of the sample was measured by Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET).
## 2.4 Electrochemical measurement
All the electrochemical measurements, including (LSV, EIS, $\mathrm{C_{dl}}$ , CP) were carried out in the electrochemical workstation (CHI 760E, CH Instrument), using $\mathrm{\sfPt}$ as the counter electrode, $\mathrm{Hg/HgO}$ electrode as reference electrode, glass carbon electrode as working electrode, 1M KOH solution as electrolyte. The working electrode was prepared by adding the $5\mathrm{mg}$ sample to a mixed solution of $490\upmu\mathrm{l}$ ethanol, $490\upmu\mathrm{l}$ ultrapure water and $20~\upmu1~5\mathrm{w\%}$ Nafion solution. Then, the mixed solution containing the catalyst sample is ultrasonic for 30 minutes to form a well-dispersed suspension. The dispersed catalyst was then dripped onto the glassy carbon electrode ( $3~\mathrm{mm}$ diameter and $0.35\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ in loading). Take $400~\upmu\mathrm{l}$ catalyst ink and add it to $1\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ foam nickel to test its stability. According to the Nernst equation $(\mathrm{E_{\(RHE)}=E_{\ (H g/H g O)}+0.924})$ , the working potential is converted into reversible hydrogen potential, and the OER overpotential is calculated by $\upeta\ \mathrm{(V)}=\mathrm{E}\ \mathrm{_{(RHE)}}{-1.23\mathrm{V}}$ . The turnover frequency (TOF) was calculated by the equation: $\mathrm{TOF}=\left(\mathrm{J}{\times}\mathrm{A}\right)/\left(4{\times}\mathrm{F}{\times}\mathrm{m}\right)$ , where J represents the current density $\mathrm{(A~cm^{-2}}$ ) at an overpotential of $300~\mathrm{{mV}}$ , A and m represent the area of the electrode and the number of moles of the active materials.
## 3. Results and discussion
XRD patterns show the diffraction peaks of $\mathrm{Fe}_{1}\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}_{1}$ -MOF in Figure 1b are consistent with those of standard MOF-74, but the intensity of peaks decreases [37], indicating the successful synthesis and relatively low crystallinity. After introducing selenium source, the diffraction peaks don’t show obvious variation, suggesting that $\mathrm{Fe_{1}C o_{2}N i_{1}S e-}$ MOF still remains the crystal structure of MOF-74. In addition, there are no obvious peaks assigned to selenides, which could be caused by the amorphous feature of selenides or low crystallinity at low temperature $(120^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ in the solvothermal process. In order to verify the formation of selenides, the samples were annealed at 600 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 1h in Ar gas atmosphere in a tube furnace to make the amorphous or lowcrystalline selenides forming a complete crystal structure. As shown in Figure 1c (the reference peaks of NiO and $\mathrm{CoO}$ are added to avoid the interference of oxides), XRD patterns of $\mathrm{Fe}_{1}\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}_{1}$ Se-MOF after high-temperature treatment, the diffraction peaks attributed to $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{Se}_{4}$ , CoSe and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ appear, which are attributed to the complete pyrolysis of MOF and crystallinity of selenides [38]. This corroborates that the successful selenylation and indirectly proves the successful synthesis of $\mathrm{Fe_{1}C o_{2}N i_{1}S e_{-}}$ MOF.
