SEM images at different magnifications were recorded to investigate the morphology evolution. As shown in Figure 2a-c, flower-like $\mathrm{Fe_{1}C o_{2}N i_{1}S e-M O F}$ is composed of self-assembled rods with the length of $1{\sim}2~{\upmu}\mathrm{m}$ and the diameter of $30{\sim}80~\mathrm{nm}$ and is decorated by many nanoparticles. In contrast, there are only durian-like aggregates for pure $\mathrm{Fe}_{1}\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}_{1}$ -MOF (Figure 2d-e) and superfine nanoparticles for $\mathrm{Fe}_{1}\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}_{1}\mathrm{Se}$ (Figure 2g-f), respectively. No rods emerge in either $\mathrm{Fe}_{1}\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}_{1}$ -MOF or $\mathrm{Fe}_{1}\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}_{1}\mathrm{Se}$ ， due to no addition of selenium source. Other samples with different compositions also show this feature as shown in Figure S2-S4, namely, upon adding selenium source, there are rods emerging in the samples. This difference suggests that the appearance of rod-shape morphology is induced by the selenylation reaction during the solvothermal process.
To distinguish the composition of rods and nanoparticles in flower-like $\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{y}\mathrm{Ni}_{z}\mathrm{Se}-$ MOF, elemental mapping from SEM equipped with energy dispersive spectrometer (EDS) was conducted. In Figure 3a-b, elemental mapping result shows that Fe, Co, Ni and Se are uniformly dispersed in the sample on the micrometer scale and the content of metal elements is Fe:Co:Ni=4.91:11.25:4.83 (Figure S1), indicating the successful selenylation. TEM observation was further employed to investigate the detailed components. Figure 3c clearly reveals the consistent morphology with SEM observation, where $\mathrm{Fe_{1}C o_{2}N i_{1}S e-M O F}$ is composed of rods and nanoparticles. The enlarged TEM image of two rods in Figure 3d shows that there are no obvious lattice fringes, indicating the low crystallinity of the rods. The selected area electron diffraction (SAED) in the inset of Figure 3d shows a faint halation, also confirming the weak crystallinity. In contrast, the nanoparticles in $\mathrm{Fe_{1}C o_{2}N i_{1}S e-M O I}$ shows random lattice fringes (Figure 3e) and a weak diffraction ring as marked by the arrows in inset of Figure 3e, which suggest that the weak crystallinity. High-resolution TEM (HRTEM) image of the marked area in Figure 3e clearly shows that there are atomic vacancies in the lattice, indicating the low crystallinity too, while the lattice spacing is ${\sim}0.26~\mathrm{nm}$ , being close to the value of (002) plane of CoSe, suggesting the formation of $\mathrm{Fe}_{1}\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}_{1}\mathrm{Se}$ .
Furthermore, the electronic structure and valence state of $\mathrm{Fe}_{1}\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}_{1}$ Se-MOF were characterized by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) (Figure 4). In the highresolution XPS spectrum of Ni 2p (Figure 4a), the peaks located at $856.4\mathrm{eV}$ and 874.3 eV in $\mathrm{Fe}_{1}\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}_{1}$ Se-MOF are attributed to $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ and $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ of $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ , respectively. Compared with pure MOF, the peaks are negatively shifted by $0.2\mathrm{eV}$ , while the peaks of $862~\mathrm{eV}$ and $880~\mathrm{eV}$ are attributed to satellite peaks (marked “Sat.”) [39]. The XPS spectrum of Co 2p (Figure 4b) in $\mathrm{Fe_{1}C o_{2}N i_{1}S e-M O F}$ at $781.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $797.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ is attributed to $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ and $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ of $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ with a negative shift by $0.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ compared to the original MOF, while the peaks at $786.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $802.6~\mathrm{eV}$ are similarly attributed to satellite peaks. In the XPS spectrum of Fe $2\mathfrak{p}$ , the peak at $711.1\mathrm{eV}$ belongs to the $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ , $713.2\mathrm{eV}$ belongs to the $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ , and the peak at $717.5\mathrm{eV}$ can be attributed to the satellite peak (Figure $\mathrm{4c}^{\cdot}$ ), which is negatively shifted by $0.3\mathrm{eV}$ compared to the original MOF. These results verify that the interfacial interaction between MOF-74 and selenides, indicating that there is electron transfer at the interface. The presence of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ is due to the partial oxidation of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ to $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ during the solvothermal process [40]. $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ can form Fe (ii)-O-Fe (iii) motifs, which can help to stabilize high valence metal sites and enhance OER activity [41]. As shown in Figure 4d, the peaks at 54.2 $\mathrm{^\circV}$ and $55.5\mathrm{eV}$ in the XPS spectrum of Se 3d are attributed to Se $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ and Se $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ , while the peaks at $59.2\mathrm{eV}$ are attributed to oxidized Se, which proves the existence of the TM-Se bond [42]. Which further proves the successful synthesis of $\mathrm{TMSe}_{2}$ in $\mathrm{Fe_{1}C o_{2}N i_{1}S e-M O F}$ . The existence of various metal ions in $\mathrm{Fe}_{1}\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}_{1}\mathrm{Se}.$ MOF would cause hybridization of electronic structures, triggering the synergistic effect to improve the catalytic performance [43-45].
The electrocatalytic properties of the as-prepared samples was tested in a standard three-electrode system by using 1M KOH solution as electrolyte and $\mathrm{Hg/HgO}$ electrode as reference electrode. Cyclic voltamogram (CV) curves in Figure 5a show that $\mathrm{Fe_{1}C o_{2}N i_{1}S e-\mathrm{N}}$ MOF exhibits an overpotential of $260~\mathrm{mV}$ and $303~\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ and $50\mathrm{\mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , respectively, both of which are superior to pure $\mathrm{Fe}_{1}\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}_{1}\mathrm{Se}$ ， $\mathrm{Fe}_{1}\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}_{1}$ -MOF and commercial $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ . The Tafel slope of $\mathrm{Fe_{1}C o_{2}N i_{1}S e-M O F}$ is only $54.3\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec^{-1}}$ , which is obviously lower than $\mathrm{Fe}_{1}\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}_{1}\mathrm{Se}$ (91.4 mV dec-1), $\mathrm{Fe}_{1}\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}_{1}.$ - MOF $\mathrm{60.7~mV~dec^{-1}}$ ) and commercial $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ ( $155.7\mathrm{mV~dec^{-1}},$ ) as shown in Figure 5b, indicating the faster reaction kinetics. Moreover, a series of $\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{y}\mathrm{Ni}_{z}\mathrm{Se}-$ MOF samples were also investigated to obtain the optimal composition as shown in Figure S5. The overpotential and Tafel slope of these samples are listed in Figure 5c, which indicates $\mathrm{Fe_{1}C o_{2}N i_{1}S e-M O F}$ is the optimal sample. We also compare the overpotential and Tafel slope reported in the literature (Figure $5\mathrm{g}$ the numbers correspond to the literature cited in support information Table S1). The results indicate our sample outperforms most of the non-noble metal electrocatalysts toward OER (Table S1).
The electron-transfer resistance $\mathrm{(R_{ct})}$ was measured by electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS). The Nyquist diagram shown in Figure 5e suggests that $\mathrm{Fe}_{1}\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}_{1}$ Se-MOF has a lower $\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{ct}},$ indicating that the electron transfer at the interface between catalyst and electrolyte is easier, which could be attributed to the charge redistribution induced by the selenylation in $\mathrm{Fe_{1}C o_{2}N i_{1}S e-M O F}$ as revealed by the XPS. The intersection with the real axis in the Nyquist diagram can provide the ohmic resistance of the material. The enlarged Nyquist diagram clearly shows that the resistance of $\mathrm{Fe_{1}C o_{2}N i_{1}S e-M O]}$ F $(8.76\Omega)$ is lower than that of $\mathrm{Fe}_{1}\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}_{1}$ -MOF (9.06 $\Omega$ ), suggesting that the selenylation improves the electrical conductivity of the electrocatalyst. The double-layer capacitance $\mathrm{(C_{dl})}$ of the samples with different elemental ratios was also measured by cyclic voltammetry (CV) to estimate the electrochemical active surface area (ECSA) (Figure S6-S9). It is noted that after selenylation the $\mathrm{C_{dl}}$ of $\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{y}\mathrm{Ni}_{z}\mathrm{Se}.$ -MOF decreases compared to $\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{y}\mathrm{Ni}_{z}$ -MOF. But the optimal sample $(\mathrm{Fe_{1}C o_{2}N i_{1}S e-M O F})$ has the largest $\mathrm{\DeltaC_{dl}}$ ( $0.42~\mathrm{mF~cm^{-2}}$ ) among all the $\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{y}\mathrm{Ni}_{z}\mathrm{Se}$ -MOF samples, indicating more active sites are exposed when the composition is 1:2:1. In order to reveal the reason for its high activity, the CV curve was normalized by ECSA. As shown in Figure S10, when the overpotential is $300\mathrm{mV}$ , $\mathrm{Fe_{1}C o_{2}N i_{1}S e-M O F}$ has the current density of $0.47\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\cm^{-2}}_{\mathrm{ECSA}}$ , indicating that it has high intrinsic activity. The material was further characterized by BET. As shown in Figure S11, the $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption-desorption curve shows that the specific surface area of $\mathrm{Fe}_{1}\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}_{1}$ -MOF is $315.2\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , while the specific surface area of Se is $117.4~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which proves that Se doping improves the intrinsic activity of the active site. In addition, the catalytic efficiency under the overpotential of $300~\mathrm{mV}$ was evaluated by TOF. As shown in Figure S12, $\mathrm{Fe}_{1}\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}_{1}$ Se-MOF has the largest TOF $(0.195\mathrm{s^{-1}}\mathrm{\dot{\Omega}}$ , which further proves its excellent intrinsic activity.
Stability is another important index to evaluate electrocatalytic performance. The catalyst was tested by chronopotentiometry at $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . As shown in Figure 5f. The initial potential increase could be caused by the reconstruction process at the surface of $\mathrm{Fe_{1}C o_{2}N i_{1}S e-M O F}$ , which commonly occurs in the selenides. It is noted that after the initial activation, the electrocatalytic stability of $\mathrm{Fe}_{1}\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Ni}_{1}\mathrm{Se}$ -MOF is very stable at 10 mA $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for 60 hours.
