The reasons for the high activity could be attributed to (ⅰ) proper introduction of Fe. $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ is partially oxidized to $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ , during the solvothermal process to form Fe (ii)- O-Fe (iii) motifs, which can stabilize the high valence active sites [41]. (ⅱ) The presence of multiple metal elements, triggering a synergistic effect of electronic structures to improve catalytic activity [44]. (ⅲ) The growth of selenides restricted by using MOF-74 as substrate, which ensures a tight interface, regulates the electrochemical environment of active transition metals at heterogeneous interfaces, and enhances the intrinsic activity of the catalyst. (ⅳ) The uniformly dispersed selenides, greatly increasing the electrochemical active area [46]. (v) Introduction of an appropriate amount of $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ , being conducive to forming more active MOF-74 and CoSe, thus enhancing the overall OER performance [47].
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, flower-like MOF-based composites was successfully synthesized by a one-step solvothermal method. The selenylation not only causes the formation of rodshape MOFs but also introduces the interfaces between MOFs and selenides as confirmed by the control experiments. The optimal $\mathrm{Fe_{1}C o_{2}N i_{1}S e-M O F}$ only needs an overpotential of $260~\mathrm{mV}$ to achieve the current density of $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , and exhibits a Tafel slope of $54.3\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec^{-1}}$ , much better than the commercial ${\mathrm{RuO}}_{2}$ and other control samples. Both the induced interfaces and the lower charge-transfer resistance contribute to the improved intrinsic activity and the fast reaction kinetics of electrocatalysts, which were corroborated by the $\mathrm{C_{dl}}$ values, XPS results and Tafel slopes. This work provides a strategy for exploring more efficient catalysts from the viewpoints of simple synthesis and earth-abundant elements.
